# Phase 2 — PySpark: Distributed Data Processing
# Consolidated Notes: Workflow, Logic & Key Concepts


## THE BIG PICTURE — WHY PHASE 2 EXISTS

Phase 1 (pandas) proved the business logic works on ONE file. Phase 2's entire
job is to make that same logic run correctly across MANY files at once
(all 7 daily CSVs together), using Spark's distributed processing — without
changing what the rules actually mean.

CORE PIPELINE SHAPE (repeated across every lab):
Raw CSVs → Rename to canonical → Clean/reject bad rows → Aggregate
(country-code grain → grid/hour grain) → Enrich with geometry
→ Tune performance → Write to disk → Wrap it all in one reusable job


## SP1 — DISTRIBUTED INGESTION

Goal: Load all 7 daily files as one Spark DataFrame, with a proper schema.

Key logic:
- Used spark.read.csv() with a MANUALLY DEFINED SCHEMA (StructType) instead of
  inferSchema=True — faster, and fails loudly instead of silently guessing
  wrong types.
- Glob pattern "sms-call-internet-mi-*.csv" — NOT the looser
  "sms-call-internet-*.csv", because the full dataset also has a different
  city's (Trentino) files that would silently double-count if the pattern
  were too loose.
- Added input_file_name() column so every row can be traced back to its
  source file.

The bug we actually hit and fixed:
The "datetime" column looked like epoch milliseconds in the trainer guide's
assumption, but the real file had it as a plain string:
"2013-11-01 00:00:00". Casting a string like that to LongType silently
produces NULL (Spark doesn't error on failed casts by default) — the fix
was using to_timestamp(col, "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss") instead of casting to a
number.

Acceptance check: distinct_hours == file_count × 24 (168 for 7 files).


## SP2 — CLEANING & STANDARDIZATION

Goal: Turn the raw DataFrame into a trusted one — same rules as NP2
(pandas), now in Spark.

Key logic — the "reject vs. handle" distinction (a recurring theme all
through this project):
- Missing grid_id / timestamp      -> REJECT (drop row) — can't assign to any grain
- Negative activity value          -> REJECT (drop row) — impossible/invalid value
- Blank (null) activity value      -> HANDLE (fill with 0) — means "no activity,"
                                        not "bad data" — documented curated-layer rule

Each of these three counts is logged SEPARATELY — never merged into one
number — because auditors/downstream consumers need to know how much data
was thrown away vs. how much was imputed.

The required proof step: a PANDAS-VS-SPARK COMPARISON TEST — ran the exact
same day's file through both the pandas UsageProcessor logic and the Spark
logic, and asserted row counts and total_activity sums matched exactly.
This exists because Spark's dropna/fillna/arithmetic can subtly differ
from pandas' — and a "close but wrong" Spark pipeline wouldn't crash, it
would just quietly produce different numbers.

Gotcha: Spark's dayofweek() = 1(Sun)...7(Sat); pandas' dt.dayofweek =
0(Mon)...6(Sun). Different conventions — not a bug, just don't compare
them directly.


## SP3 — NETWORK ACTIVITY AGGREGATIONS (THE GRAIN TRANSITION)

Goal: Collapse many country-code rows into ONE row per grid per hour.

Why this is called "the single most important validation point in the
whole programme":
The raw/canonical grain is timestamp + grid_id + country_code (many rows
per grid/hour). Every downstream number — KPIs, ML features, API
responses, dashboard charts — assumes one row per grid per hour. If this
aggregation is subtly wrong (e.g., missing a groupBy key), you get
duplicate rows at the wrong grain, which SILENTLY INFLATES every number
downstream with no error anywhere.

The logic:
    hourly_grid_summary = clean_df.groupBy("grid_id","timestamp","date",
                                            "hour","day_of_week")
                                   .agg(sum of each activity column)

country_code is deliberately dropped here — it does not belong past this
point.

Built-in hard assertions (not just eyeballed once):
- Zero duplicates on (grid_id, timestamp) after aggregation
- Output row count < input row count (proves aggregation actually reduced rows)
- country_code absent from the result

We hand-verified this with real numbers — summed the individual
country-code rows for one grid/hour by hand and confirmed it matched the
aggregated row exactly, column by column.


## SP4 — GEOSPATIAL ENRICHMENT (JOINING ACTIVITY TO GEOGRAPHY)

Goal: Attach real map geometry (from milano-grid.geojson) to each grid_id.

The critical trap: every GeoJSON feature has TWO different IDs:
- top-level "id"        -> 0-indexed (starts at 0)
- properties.cellId     -> 1-indexed (starts at 1, matches grid_id's real range)

Using the wrong one (id instead of cellId) produces a join that reports
100% coverage and looks completely fine — but every single grid's
activity would render on its NEIGHBOR's polygon on a map. Nothing errors,
nothing crashes. THIS IS WHY A NUMERIC COVERAGE PERCENTAGE IS NOT
SUFFICIENT PROOF THE JOIN IS CORRECT — we also had to spot-check real
coordinates.

The logic:
1. Load GeoJSON with plain Python json (not Spark — file is small, only
   10,000 features)
2. Flatten properties.cellId -> grid_id + compute a CENTROID (average of
   polygon corner points) for each grid
3. LEFT JOIN (not inner) — a left join keeps every row and turns
   non-matches into visible NULLs you can count; an inner join would
   silently drop unmatched rows
4. Originally used broadcast() since the lookup is tiny (10k rows vs
   1.68M) — copies the small table to every worker, avoiding an expensive
   shuffle. (Had to remove this on this machine due to a local environment
   issue — see Environment Issues section below.)

Validation — two layers, not just one:
- NUMERIC: row count unchanged after join (proves no duplicate keys in the
  lookup), 0 unmatched grids, 100% coverage
- GEOGRAPHIC (the real proof): printed grid 1 and grid 2's actual
  centroids and confirmed they're (a) inside Milan's real bounding box
  (lon ~9.0–9.3, lat ~45.35–45.55), and (b) close to each other but NOT
  identical — proving genuinely distinct, correctly-mapped adjacent cells.


## SP5 — PERFORMANCE & EXECUTION BEHAVIOUR

Goal: Learn to read EVIDENCE before optimizing — not apply every trick
blindly.

Four experiments, each teaching a different concept:

1. explain() on the aggregation
   -> Showed Spark's physical plan — filter pushdown to the CSV scan
      itself, partial-then-final HashAggregate pattern, the shuffle
      (Exchange) step.
   -> Educational — confirms Spark is already doing sensible things
      automatically.

2. Cache a reused DataFrame
   -> Without cache: ~11–13s every repeated .count(). With cache: first
      call slower (~22s, builds the cache), then 0.3s on repeats — 30x+
      faster.
   -> ACCEPTED — but noted memory spillover to disk on this machine (logs
      showed "Not enough space to cache in memory").

3. Repartition by "date" vs. force 200 partitions
   -> By-date: 7 partitions, 2.97s. Forced 200: 5.30s — SLOWER, not
      faster.
   -> REJECTED the 200-partition idea — on local[2] (only 2 real
      workers), more partitions than workers just adds coordination
      overhead with no parallel gain.

4. Column pruning before groupBy
   -> Full columns: 2.51s. Pruned to only needed columns: 2.19s.
   -> ACCEPTED as a cheap habit — less data through the shuffle.

The real lesson: caching helped a lot, over-partitioning on a small local
setup actively hurt — proving "more partitions/more parallelism" is not
automatically better. Evidence beats folklore.


## SP6 — WRITE PROCESSED & ANALYTICS DATA

Goal: Persist Spark's output in the right format for each use case — not
just print results in a notebook.

Three outputs, three different reasons:

1. Cleaned activity -> PARQUET, partitioned by date
   Columnar + compressed — far more efficient for analytics; partitioning
   lets later queries skip irrelevant date folders entirely.

2. hourly_grid_summary -> PARQUET, no geometry column
   Geometry belongs in the static reference layer (10k rows), not
   duplicated into every one of 1.68M fact rows — this is the
   fact-vs-dimension design principle that Phase 3's warehouse modeling
   will formalize.

3. Dashboard summary -> CSV, single file (coalesce(1))
   Tiny, human-readable summary meant for quick inspection (e.g. opening
   in Excel) — CSV is the right tool here, for the opposite reason
   Parquet was right above.

Required round-trip validation: read the Parquet back and re-check row
counts, schema, and the zero-duplicates assertion STILL HOLD after a
write+read cycle — proving the write itself didn't subtly corrupt
anything.

Real measured result: raw CSVs = 631.6 MB -> Parquet = 165.1 MB (26% of
original size) — the concrete, measured payoff of columnar storage +
compression, not just a theoretical claim.


## SP7 — BUILD THE REUSABLE SPARK ETL JOB

Goal: Turn SP1–SP6 into ONE production-style script — telecom_pipeline.py
— with proper functions, configuration, logging, and fail-fast behavior.

The five functions (single responsibility each, same principle as NP2's
pandas class):
- read_raw()      -> load + rename to canonical, fail loudly if no files found
- clean()         -> reject bad rows, null-to-zero rule, derive time features
- aggregate()     -> THE grain transition (country_code -> grid/hour), with
                     hard assertions
- enrich()        -> join to geometry, validate (not written to disk —
                     geometry stays separate)
- write_outputs() -> Parquet + CSV writes, matching SP6's design
- main()          -> wires it all together, structured logging, non-zero
                     exit on failure

Nothing hardcoded — --input-dir, --reference-dir, --output-dir are all
command-line arguments, so the exact same script can run against any
environment/folder without code changes.

Fail-fast, proven with a real test: running against an empty folder
produced a clear error message ("NO INPUT FILES FOUND...") AND exited
with code 1 (confirmed via $LASTEXITCODE) — this matters because an
orchestrator (coming in Phase 3) checks exit codes, not just log text, to
detect failure.

Final verification — every number matched every prior lab exactly:
input_rows=15,089,165, rejected=0/0, nulls_handled=48,562,194,
output_rows=1,679,994 — identical to SP2/SP3/SP6's independently-verified
numbers. This proves the "refactor" genuinely reused the same logic
rather than silently reimplementing it differently.


## RECURRING THEMES ACROSS ALL OF PHASE 2

1. Silent failures are the real danger, not crashes. A wrong grain, a
   wrong identifier, a bad cast — none of these throw an error. They just
   produce plausible-looking wrong numbers. Every lab's validation step
   exists specifically to catch this class of bug.
2. Reject vs. handle is a business decision, not a technical one —
   missing keys and negative values are invalid (reject); blank activity
   is meaningful (handle/zero-fill). Always logged as separate counts.
3. Grain matters more than almost anything else. Getting groupBy right
   (SP3) is the single highest-leverage correctness check in the whole
   pipeline.
4. Coverage % alone never proves a join is correct — always paired with a
   real, spot-checkable fact (geographic coordinates, hand-summed
   totals).
5. Evidence over folklore for performance — measure before you optimize,
   and be willing to reject a "best practice" that doesn't hold up on
   your actual setup.


## ENVIRONMENT ISSUES WE SOLVED (useful to remember for later phases)

- Missing JAVA_HOME -> Spark can't reliably launch its internal
  processes. Fixed by setting it explicitly (either system-wide or via
  os.environ[...] at the top of each script).
- Python 3.14 too new for PySpark -> created a dedicated virtual
  environment (venv) running Python 3.11, kept separate from the main
  system Python (Phase 1/pandas work is unaffected).
- local[*] caused "Python worker failed to connect back" -> this
  machine's antivirus/corporate security software likely throttles
  simultaneous process creation. Fixed by using local[2] (or local[1])
  instead — trades some speed for reliability, with zero effect on
  correctness.